WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.385
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:15.379
Unit twenty: History of Science. Today you will give background to discoveries with the past perfect.

4
00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:18.192
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.169
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.017 --> 00:00:23.971
Science.

7
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:29.549
Science means systematic study of the natural world through observation and experiment.

8
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.380
Science advanced rapidly in the 17th century.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.299
Your turn. Say: Science.

10
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.262
Science.

11
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:41.983
Discovery.

12
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:45.610
Discovery means the act of finding something new.

13
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:48.832
The discovery of penicillin saved millions.

14
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:51.814
Your turn. Say: Discovery.

15
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:55.761
Discovery.

16
00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:57.715
Revolution.

17
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.260
Revolution means a dramatic change in ideas or methods.

18
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.755
The Scientific Revolution transformed knowledge.

19
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.886
Your turn. Say: Revolution.

20
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:12.723
Revolution.

21
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:14.392
Paradigm.

22
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:18.474
Paradigm means a framework of accepted theories.

23
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:21.756
Copernicus challenged the existing paradigm.

24
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.072
Your turn. Say: Paradigm.

25
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:28.969
Paradigm.

26
00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:30.973
Experiment.

27
00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:35.423
Experiment means a test carried out to check a hypothesis.

28
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:38.829
Galileo's experiments tested falling objects.

29
00:01:39.417 --> 00:01:42.069
Your turn. Say: Experiment.

30
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:46.027
Experiment.

31
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:47.587
Theory.

32
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:51.279
Theory means a well-supported explanation.

33
00:01:51.883 --> 00:01:54.916
Darwin's theory explained the variety of species.

34
00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:57.960
Your turn. Say: Theory.

35
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.639
Theory.

36
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:03.408
Instrument.

37
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:07.275
Instrument means a tool for scientific measurement.

38
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:10.661
The telescope was a revolutionary instrument.

39
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.508
Your turn. Say: Instrument.

40
00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.263
Instrument.

41
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:19.029
Knowledge.

42
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:23.572
Knowledge means understanding gained through study or experience.

43
00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:26.879
Scientific knowledge is always open to revision.

44
00:02:27.483 --> 00:02:29.811
Your turn. Say: Knowledge.

45
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:33.679
Knowledge.

46
00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:38.512
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.196
Visal, did astronomy really begin with Galileo's telescope?

48
00:02:44.283 --> 00:02:55.945
Not at all. Scientists had observed the planets for thousands of years before the telescope was invented, in Babylon, India and China.

49
00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.851
So what made Galileo's work revolutionary?

50
00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:09.988
Earlier astronomers had assumed that Earth was the centre. Copernicus had already proposed otherwise, and the telescope gave new evidence.

51
00:03:11.083 --> 00:03:13.338
Any examples closer to home?

52
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:22.835
Khmer engineers had designed sophisticated water systems at Angkor long before modern hydrology had developed as a science.

53
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.872
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:29.958
Grammar focus: Past perfect for background.

55
00:03:30.258 --> 00:03:31.469
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.183 --> 00:03:36.470
Scientists had observed the planets long before the telescope was invented.

57
00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:42.425
Earlier astronomers had assumed Earth was the centre before Galileo published his findings.

58
00:03:43.317 --> 00:03:48.346
Khmer engineers had designed complex reservoirs long before hydrology became a science.

59
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:59.130
Use the past perfect (had + p.p.) for an action completed before another past moment. It sets the background for the main past event, which is usually in the past simple.

60
00:03:59.883 --> 00:04:03.859
Be careful! Don't say: They had observe the planets before.

61
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.705
Say: They had observed the planets before.

62
00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:11.399
Past perfect: had + p.p.

63
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.585
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.807
Before the Revolution.

65
00:04:17.107 --> 00:04:23.252
Histories of science once presented progress as a series of sudden breakthroughs by European geniuses.

66
00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:30.933
Yet by the time of the Scientific Revolution, scholars in the Islamic world had already preserved and extended Greek knowledge.

67
00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:37.532
Indian mathematicians had developed the decimal system and the concept of zero centuries earlier.

68
00:04:38.533 --> 00:04:44.341
When Copernicus proposed a sun-centred system, he had studied the work of many earlier astronomers.

69
00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:51.896
Today historians emphasise that scientific knowledge has always been built collectively, across many cultures.

70
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.060
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.917 --> 00:05:02.029
By 1600, astronomers blank the planets for centuries.

72
00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:07.155
A, had observed. B, have observed. C, observed had.

73
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.734
The answer is A: had observed.

74
00:05:13.883 --> 00:05:17.891
Before Galileo, Copernicus blank a sun-centred model.

75
00:05:18.191 --> 00:05:22.390
A, has proposed. B, had proposed. C, proposes.

76
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:28.386
The answer is B: had proposed.

77
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.956
A framework of accepted theories is a blank.

78
00:05:32.256 --> 00:05:36.234
A, discovery. B, instrument. C, paradigm.

79
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.916
The answer is C: paradigm.

80
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.073
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:45.373 --> 00:05:50.524
A, make up an experiment. B, conduct an experiment. C, take an experiment.

82
00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:57.041
The answer is B: conduct an experiment.

83
00:05:58.183 --> 00:06:01.715
According to the reading, scientific knowledge has always been…

84
00:06:02.015 --> 00:06:07.167
A, built collectively. B, invented by one genius. C, European only.

85
00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.268
The answer is A: built collectively.

86
00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:17.816
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.892
Which scientific discovery changed history most?

88
00:06:38.683 --> 00:06:40.207
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.507 --> 00:07:15.478
I think the discovery of germs as the cause of disease changed history more than any other. Before Pasteur and Koch, doctors had believed that illnesses spread through 'bad air', so many treatments were ineffective. Once germ theory was accepted, hygiene, vaccines and antibiotics saved hundreds of millions of lives. Some people would choose electricity or the printing press instead. Those were vital too, but without germ theory, far fewer people would have survived to benefit from them.

90
00:07:15.778 --> 00:07:17.661
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:23.141
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past perfect for background.

92
00:07:23.441 --> 00:07:27.510
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

93
00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.323
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:30.623 --> 00:07:33.438
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

